What is a Branching Strategy?
A branching strategy is a defined workflow or set of rules that teams follow to manage branches in a Git repository. It ensures smooth collaboration, structured code development, efficient testing, and safe deployments across multiple developers and environments.

🔀 Why Use a Branching Strategy?
•Maintain code stability.
•Enable parallel development.
•Reduce merge conflicts.
•Enforce code review and CI/CD gates.
•Provide rollback options.

🔁 Common Branching Strategies in DevOps
1. Git Flow (Feature Driven)-Great for large teams and structured release cycles.
🎗️Branches Used:
-main
-develop
-feature/*
-release/*
-hotfix/*
🔄 Workflow:
•Dev creates feature/login from develop
•After completion, merge into develop
•Create release/v1.0 from develop
•QA & test → merge into main and tag as release
•Any emergency fix? Create hotfix/critical-fix from main, then merge into both main and develop
👍 Pros: Structured, great for teams
 👎 Cons: Can be complex for smaller project

2. Trunk-Based Development-Common in CI/CD environments with frequent releases (e.g., startups, fast-paced teams)
🎗️ Branches Used:
-main (trunk)
-short-lived (feature) branches (merged within hours or a day)
🔄 Workflow:
•Dev creates feature-xyz, finishes work, and merges to main the same day
•Automated tests and CI/CD pipelines run on every push to main
👍 Pros: Fast, modern, CI/CD friendly
 👎 Cons: Needs strong testing & automation

3. GitHub Flow-Simpler version of Git Flow, ideal for cloud-native and open-source projects
🎗️ Branches Used:
-main
-feature branches (with pull requests)
🔄 Workflow:
•Create a branch (feature/login)
•Push and create a Pull Request (PR)
•Review → Merge to main → Trigger deployment
👍 Pros: Lightweight and PR-driven
 👎 Cons: Less structured for releases

✅ What is a Pull Request (PR)?
A Pull Request (PR) is a request to merge code changes from one branch into another—typically from a feature branch into the main or develop branch—after review and approval. 
🔍 Why Pull Requests Are Important?
•Code Review: Allows team members to review and discuss changes before merging.
•Collaboration: Encourages communication between developers.
•Quality Check: Helps maintain code quality through testing and approvals.
•Audit Trail: Keeps a history of changes, comments, and approvals for reference.

📘 In GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, etc.
•Called Pull Request in GitHub & Bitbucket.
•Called Merge Request (MR) in GitLab.

🛠 Typical Pull Request Workflow:
 Create a branch → make changes → push code → open a PR → get it reviewed → get it approved → merge into main.

✅ Who Approves a Pull Request?
A Pull Request (PR) is typically approved by reviewers assigned from your team, based on the project's rules and team structure.
